  "full_response": "### **How Vector Databases Work**\nVector databases are specialized databases designed to store, index, and query **high-dimensional vector embeddings**—numerical representations of data (e.g., text, images, audio) generated by machine learning models like **transformers, CNNs, or word2vec**. Unlike traditional databases that rely on exact matches (e.g., SQL queries), vector databases enable **semantic similarity search** by comparing vectors based on their mathematical proximity in a multi-dimensional space.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Components of Vector Databases**\n1. **Vector Embeddings**\n   - Data (e.g., text, images) is converted into dense vectors using models like:\n     - **Text**: Sentence-BERT, OpenAI’s `text-embedding-ada-002`\n     - **Images**: ResNet, CLIP\n     - **Audio**: VGGish, Wav2Vec\n   - Example: The sentence *\"The cat sits on the mat\"* might be encoded as a 384-dimensional vector.\n\n2. **Indexing for Fast Search**\n   - Vectors are stored in optimized indexes to enable sub-linear search time. Common indexing methods:\n     - **Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) Algorithms**:\n       - **HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)**: Graph-based, balances speed/accuracy.\n       - **IVF (Inverted File Index)**: Clusters vectors into \"buckets\" for faster scanning.\n       - **PQ (Product Quantization)**: Compresses vectors to reduce memory usage.\n       - **LSH (Locality-Sensitive Hashing)**: Hashes similar vectors to the same \"buckets.\"\n\n3. **Similarity Metrics**\n   - Vectors are compared using distance metrics:\n     - **Cosine Similarity**: Measures the angle between vectors (common for text).\n     - **Euclidean Distance (L2)**: Straight-line distance (common for images).\n     - **Dot Product**: Scales with vector magnitude (used in recommendation systems).\n\n4. **Query Execution**\n   - A query (e.g., *\"A feline resting on a rug\"*) is converted into a vector.\n   - The database retrieves the **top-*k* most similar vectors** from the index using ANN search.\n\n---\n\n### **Role in Semantic Search**\nSemantic search aims to understand the **meaning** behind queries, not just keyword matches. Vector databases enable this by:\n\n1. **Capturing Contextual",
